      <title>Current Sensors</title>
      <link>//localhost:1313/iot/esp/components/current-sensors/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>//localhost:1313/iot/esp/components/current-sensors/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div id=&#34;van-list-0&#34; class=&#34;van-img-list van-img-list-small&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;script type=&#34;module&#34;&gt;&#xA;import { renderImgYtList } from &#39;/js/img-yt-list.js&#39;;&#xA;&#xA;try {&#xA;  renderImgYtList(&#39;van-list-0&#39;, &#34;\n\n- title: INA219\n  href: https:\/\/www.amazon.ca\/INA219-Bi-Directional-Current-Breakout-Monitoring\/dp\/B0CTG82S1B\/ref=sr_1_1?crid=IT29SWOTV6OP\u0026dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.K-7lpGyTxfh1_Z7pwoqUdaH2O8KhFtBw_PCDSndFYADZ9mnX5VoH8upHy_ZoEGr3mFFdX2T_wG8ILCbyqqM-BpPICYajPYs0jhkv35yvfWdyzZGMr3oJyT2e31NVvfhNf-gwelwWfjtOxgxIQNR8DJF-luSCBTvYi6FspvVnXbWPWEPVP4MGEU_aPLfe6ny38wf67x6dVa3AdcGpGIw40LzH40aGFcfdPQr2sLF1KJ384hw2jlL-aCxkdJtcZdJT5XagRqaWvmWUIDA_7CPfOg-zS3fc-h9LLtYk3FyhndA.OF35iNtW_oSoU7s_ePUYbwPpNO0UMSHbgX-Du_5eMVc\u0026dib_tag=se\u0026keywords=ina219\u0026qid=1778016350\u0026sprefix=ina219%2Caps%2C231\u0026sr=8-1)\n  img: ..\/ina219.jpg\n  text: |\n    I2C Zero Drift Bi-directional Current Power Supply Sensor Breakout\n\n    Specifications：\n    * 1.0.1 ohm 1% 2W current sense resistor\n    * 2.Up to \u002b26V target voltage\n    * 3.Up to ±3.2A current measurement, with ±0.8mA resolution\n    * 4.0.9\u0022 x 0.8\u0022 PCB\n    * 5.This board\/chip uses 12C 7-bit addresses Ox40, Ox41, Ox44, Ox45, selectable with jumpers\n\n    Package Included:\n    2pcs INA219 Sensor Module\n\n    Examples:\n    * [AdaFruit](https:\/\/learn.adafruit.com\/adafruit-ina219-current-sensor-breakout\/overview)\n    * [Autodesk Instructables](https:\/\/www.instructables.com\/INA219-Arduino-Based-Wattmeter\/)\n    * [Waveshare demo](https:\/\/www.waveshare.com\/wiki\/Tutorial_VII:_INA219_Voltage_And_Current_Monitoring_Demo)\n\n- title: INA226\n  img: ..\/ina226.jpg\n  href: https:\/\/www.amazon.ca\/CANADUINO-INA226-Precision-Current-Monitor\/dp\/B088D8Y7G8\/ref=pd_sbs_d_sccl_2_5\/143-7637609-1794059?pd_rd_w=g3HNK\u0026content-id=amzn1.sym.4296b8ba-ef9b-4a5b-8472-6a970437f86d\u0026pf_rd_p=4296b8ba-ef9b-4a5b-8472-6a970437f86d\u0026pf_rd_r=SWHB07WJZA7X2E9HT0HV\u0026pd_rd_wg=YERQp\u0026pd_rd_r=0810cb67-a928-4c00-91a5-38aa9bb7b7c4\u0026pd_rd_i=B088D8Y7G8\u0026psc=1)\n  text: |\n    The INA226 Current Sensor is a current shunt and power monitor with an I2C compatible interface. The device monitors both a shunt voltage drop and bus supply voltage. Programmable calibration value, conversion times, and averaging, combined with an internal multiplier, enable direct readouts of current in amperes and power in watts.\n\n    The INA226 Current Sensor is used to measure current on common-mode bus voltages that can vary from 0 V to 36 V, independent of the supply voltage. The device operates from a single 2.7-V to 5.5-V supply, drawing a typical of 330μA of supply current. It is specified over the operating temperature range between –40°C and 125°C and features up to 16 programmable addresses on the I2C-compatible interface.\n\n    * Senses Bus Voltages From 0 V to 36 V\n    * Reports Current, Voltage, and Power\n    * High-Side or Low-Side Sensing\n    * High Accuracy: 0.1% max. Gain Error, 10μV max. Offset\n    * Configurable Averaging Options\n    * 16 Programmable Addresses\n    * Operates from 2.7-V to 5.5-V Power Supply\n    * Shunt voltage -82mV to \u002b82mV\n\n    *The factory-assembled shunt resistor on the INA226 Current Sensor Module of 0.1 Ohm enables a measuring range of about -820 to \u002b820mA. For a higher measuring range, the shunt resistor can be replaced with a different value. Please refer to the datasheet for details.\n\n    Library and code examples available on GitHub\n\n    Examples\n\n    * [Cirkit Designer](https:\/\/docs.cirkitdesigner.com\/component\/c1dd9b75-76f4-4953-8343-31bb2daa2cc8\/ina226) : How to Use INA226: Examples, Pinouts, and Specs\n    * [DeepBluEmbedded](https:\/\/deepbluembedded.com\/ina226-with-arduino-dc-current-sensor-ina226-library-code\/) INA226 With Arduino (DC Current Sensor) | INA226 Library \u002b Code\n\n&#34;,&#xA;    {&#xA;      size: &#34;small&#34;,&#xA;      circle: &#34;false&#34;,&#xA;      shadow: &#34;false&#34;,&#xA;      blank: &#34;true&#34;&#xA;    }&#xA;  );&#xA;} catch (e) {&#xA;  console.error(&#39;Error rendering van-list:&#39;, e);&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/script&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>DC-DC</title>
      <link>//localhost:1313/iot/esp/components/dc-dc/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>//localhost:1313/iot/esp/components/dc-dc/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div id=&#34;van-list-0&#34; class=&#34;van-img-list van-img-list-small&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;script type=&#34;module&#34;&gt;&#xA;import { renderImgYtList } from &#39;/js/img-yt-list.js&#39;;&#xA;&#xA;try {&#xA;  renderImgYtList(&#39;van-list-0&#39;, &#34;\n\n- title: LM2596 Buck Converter\n  href: https:\/\/www.amazon.ca\/LM2596-Converter-3-0-40V-1-5-35V-Supply\/dp\/B08NV3JCBC\n  img: ..\/lm2596.jpg\n  text: |\n\n    * DC-DC step down buck converter.\n    * Input voltage: 4-40V\n    * Output Voltage: 1.25-35V (less than input voltage)\n    * Max Current: 3A (may need additional heat sink)\n    * 5 pack\n\n    [AutoDesk Instructables: How to Use DC to DC Buck Converter LM2596](https:\/\/www.instructables.com\/How-to-Use-DC-to-DC-Buck-Converter-LM2596\/)\n\n- title: MT3608\n  href: https:\/\/www.amazon.ca\/AIHJCNELE-Converter-Adjustable-MT3608-Transformer\/dp\/B09Z358PVZ?th=1\n  img: ..\/mt3608.jpg\n  text: |\n    This product adds a Micro USB interface on the basis of the ordinary MT3608 module, which can be directly applied to the charger as the input terminal. (4pcs)\n\n    * Rectification method: asynchronous rectification\n    * Input voltage: DC 2 V - 24 V\n    * Output Voltage: DC 5V - 28V\n    * Maximum output current: 2A\n    * Efficiency: 93%\n    * Switching frequency: 1.2 M.Hz\n    * Output ripple: less than 100mV\n    * Load Regulation: 0.5%\n    * Voltage regulation rate: 0.5%\n    * (Minimum) (input-output) difference voltage when input is at (2 V and 50% of max-input-V), loading at (50% and 100% rated current)\n\n    [AutoDesk Instructables: DC-DC Boost Converter MT3608](https:\/\/www.instructables.com\/DC-DC-Boost-Converter-MT3608\/)\n\n&#34;,&#xA;    {&#xA;      size: &#34;small&#34;,&#xA;      circle: &#34;false&#34;,&#xA;      shadow: &#34;false&#34;,&#xA;      blank: &#34;true&#34;&#xA;    }&#xA;  );&#xA;} catch (e) {&#xA;  console.error(&#39;Error rendering van-list:&#39;, e);&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/script&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>HC-SR04</title>
      <link>//localhost:1313/iot/esp/components/hc-sr04/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>//localhost:1313/iot/esp/components/hc-sr04/</guid>
      <description>&lt;div id=&#34;van-list-0&#34; class=&#34;van-img-list van-img-list-small&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;script type=&#34;module&#34;&gt;&#xA;import { renderImgYtList } from &#39;/js/img-yt-list.js&#39;;&#xA;&#xA;try {&#xA;  renderImgYtList(&#39;van-list-0&#39;, &#34;\n\n- title: HC-SR04\n  href: https:\/\/www.amazon.ca\/DIYables-HC-SR04-Ultrasonic-Arduino-Raspberry\/dp\/B0BDFLPZ2R\/ref=sr_1_7?crid=PMS2RVJADYDR\u0026dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.s4AQIZwGwmYgx526ukMmCLgT85Y85SgfQRJNWs4v1QT5gEfH_axcFyes9JNm6n12z205NURXAsMhhvPG8ZL_R5o9MXgFkoJoeoFWZuFnBAzqmOTlehDWpySZqYEulEFeHNxwj6fef1x_VcuP-_KEVw3_N9VsfKkBzSjDSilfL7d3XnL6yf7pHUro3vUfDGoPGUOWeHo7D81a9LDsc5PZEeeeB-XF8riVWvGzZMIXhiK9ppSOWpWZJOvvmLFaeS-z_53DdBA43bTEIeJmKJ6sWpHKT03DNxNXkuhnZBf0ASA.WzdMjtFVdhNUdjD_rdnR6ToPXZkxmu2DwRoIQtjVieI\u0026dib_tag=se\u0026keywords=hc-sr40\u0026qid=1778023722\u0026sprefix=hc-sr40%2Caps%2C162\u0026sr=8-7\n  img: ..\/hc-sr40.jpg\n  text: |\n    This package includes two pieces of HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Sensor that work with Arduino, ESP32, ESP8266, Raspberry Pi, or any 5V or 3.3V microcontroller. This sensor can be used to measure the distance to objects or used for obstacle avoidance.\n\n    SPECIFICATION\n    - Operating Voltage: 5V\n    - Logic voltage: 3.3V or 5V\n    - Trigger Input Pulse width: 10uS\n    - Static current: Less than 2mA\n    - Sensor angle: Less than 15 degrees\n    - Detection distance: 2cm-450cm\n    - High precision: Up to 3MM\n    - Echo signal Output: TTL PWL signal\n    - Operating frequency: 40Hz\n    - Color: Blue\n    - Size: 45*20*15 mm\n    - Weight: 10g\n\n    TUTORIALS:\n    - Arduino and ESP32 online tutorials: DIYables Ultrasonic Sensor\n\n&#34;,&#xA;    {&#xA;      size: &#34;small&#34;,&#xA;      circle: &#34;false&#34;,&#xA;      shadow: &#34;false&#34;,&#xA;      blank: &#34;true&#34;&#xA;    }&#xA;  );&#xA;} catch (e) {&#xA;  console.error(&#39;Error rendering van-list:&#39;, e);&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Demo&lt;/p&gt;</description>
